// Diag snippet: a literal constraint in a struct pattern is DROPPED.
//
// `is P { x: 10, name }` should match only when `x == 10`. It matches any `P`:
// the lowered AST holds no trace of the `10` at all, so the arm is compiled as
// a bare `is P`. VM and native agree, which is what makes it a lowering bug
// rather than a backend one.
//
// Expected once fixed:
//   x=10 -> matched ten, name=bob
//   x=99 -> fell through
// Actual today: both lines report "matched ten".
//
// Found 2026-09-21 while validating W0014 — the warning fired on
// `match_struct_pattern_binding`'s wildcard, because the constrained arm
// subtracts the whole of `P` from the remaining set. T3013 hides the
// consequence on a non-union scrutinee (it demands a wildcard regardless), so
// the drop only becomes visible on a union, where the match type-checks as
// exhaustive AND takes the wrong arm.

module "snippet"

import "std/io"

P :: struct { x: i32, name: string }

main :: fn() -> i32 {
    for v in [10, 99] {
        p :: P { .x = v, .name = "bob" }
        match p {
            is P { x: 10, name } -> println("x=${v} -> matched ten, name=${name}")
            _                    -> println("x=${v} -> fell through")
        }
    }
    return 0
}
